  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 4,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.046,
            0.364,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.087,
            0.846,
            0.124
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The required point C is one of the points of intersection of the two circles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.125,
            0.53,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The reason why the point of intersection is the third vertex is the same as for equilateral triangles. This point lies on both the circles. Hence its distance from A is the radius of the circle centred at A (5 cm) and its distance from B is the same as the radius of the circle centred at B (6 cm)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.273,
            0.529,
            0.346
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us summarise the steps of construction, noting that constructing full circles is not necessary to get the third vertex (see Fig. 7.2 and 7.3)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.539,
            0.115,
            0.848,
            0.321
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.539,
                0.115,
                0.848,
                0.321
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.351,
            0.846,
            0.39
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "AB = 4 cm (see Fig. 7.1)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.395,
            0.848,
            0.433
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Step 2:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " From A, construct a sufficiently long arc of radius 5 cm (see Fig. 7.2)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.438,
            0.848,
            0.476
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Step 3:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " From B, construct an arc of radius 6 cm such that it intersects the first arc (see Fig. 7.3)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.48,
            0.847,
            0.52
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Step 4:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The point where both the arcs meet is the required third vertex C. Join AC and BC to get ∆ABC."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.538,
            0.279,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Construct"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.567,
            0.846,
            0.607
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Construct triangles having the following sidelengths (all the units are in cm):"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.617,
            0.292,
            0.637
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(a) 4, 4, 6"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.641,
            0.292,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(b) 3, 4, 5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.665,
            0.292,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(c) 1, 5, 5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.689,
            0.292,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(d) 4, 6, 8"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.713,
            0.341,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(e) 3.5, 3.5, 3.5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.742,
            0.848,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have seen that triangles having all three equal sides are called equilateral triangles. Those having two equal sides are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "isosceles triangles",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.808,
            0.293,
            0.831
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure it Out"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.834,
            0.695,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Use the points on the circle and/or the centre to form isosceles triangles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 19,
